2. Search for login pages of Zomato by using Google dorking techniques. Write the search query you used and explain why it works.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Try using 'inurl:login site:zomato.com'.</em>
=>inurl:login site:zomato.com
Why it works:
site:zomato.com restricts Google’s index to only pages hosted on that domain.
inurl:login filters those results down to URLs that literally contain the string “login” in the path (e.g., /login, /auth/login).
Combined, this quickly surfaces the authentication entry point without manually browsing the site.

3. Find exposed Excel files containing the word 'playlist' from Spotify-related domains using Google hacking methods. Share your search query and one result (URL) you found.
>filetype:xlsx "playlist" site:spotify.com
or more broadly, without restricting to one domain:
filetype:xls OR filetype:xlsx "playlist" intext:spotify
Why this pattern is used in real Google-hacking databases: filetype: narrows results to spreadsheet files, and a keyword like "playlist" targets files whose content (not just filename) mentions it — useful because organizations sometimes accidentally leave internal spreadsheets indexable if they’re uploaded to a public-facing storage bucket or CMS without access controls.

4.Identify possible exposed admin panels for any Indian e-commerce site (like Flipkart or Myntra) using Google hacking. Write the dork you used and explain what makes such panels risky if left open.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use 'intitle:admin site:flipkart.com' or similar.</em>
=>The dork pattern:
intitle:admin site:flipkart.com
or variants like:
inurl:admin intitle:"login" site:myntra.com
Why it works, mechanically: intitle: matches text in the page’s <title> tag (admin panels are frequently titled generically, e.g., “Admin Login” or “Dashboard”), which, combined with site:, narrows the hunt to one organization’s indexed pages only.
Why such panels are risky if actually left open:
Admin panels usually offer far more privilege than a customer login — user data access, inventory/pricing control, order management, sometimes even database or server-level actions.
If an admin panel is accidentally indexable by Google, it often means it also lacks IP allow-listing, VPN gating, or other network-level protections — meaning anyone who finds the URL can attempt to log in, not just view it.
Combined with weak/default/reused admin credentials (common in smaller deployments), an exposed panel turns a “just visible” issue into a full account/data compromise very quickly.
This is why security teams run their own periodic Google-dork audits of their domains — to catch these before attackers do.
